Mobile Guards: A Variation on Chvatal’s Theorem,\n\nGeometriae Dedicata 14 (1983), 273-283.\n\nUniversity of Wroc law, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, pl.\n\nGrunwaldzki 2, 50-384 Wroc law, Poland\n\nE-mail address:marek.szykula@ii.uni.wroc.pl, andrzej.kisielewicz@math.uni.wroc.pl\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Independent Spanning Trees in Random and Pseudorandom Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.26401)\nciteturn6academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; This fundamental problem has received much attention, in part motivated by its applications to network reliability, but despite that has only been resolved for $k \\le 4$ and certain restricted graph families. ... In addition, we show that $(n,d,\\lambda)$-graphs with fairly mild bounds on the spectral ratio $d/\\lambda$ contain $(1-o(1))d$ independent spanning trees rooted at each vertex, thereby settling the conjecture asymptotically for random $d$-regular graphs as well.\nTitle: On Independent Spanning Trees in Random and Pseudorandom Graphs\nAuthors: Nemanja Draganić, Keith Frankston, Michael Krivelevich, Alexey Pokrovskiy, Liana Yepremyan\nDate: Tue Sep 30 15:33:11 2025\n\nIn 1989, Zehavi and Itai conjectured that every $k$-connected graph contains $k$ independent spanning trees rooted at any prescribed vertex $r$. That is, for each vertex $v$, the unique $r$-$v$ paths within these $k$ spanning trees are internally disjoint. This fundamental problem has received much attention, in part motivated by its applications to network reliability, but despite that has only been resolved for $k \\le 4$ and certain restricted graph families. We establish the conjecture for almost all graphs of essentially any relevant density. Specifically, we prove that there exists a constant $C > 1$ such that, with high probability, the random graph $G(n,p)$ contains $\\delta(G)$ independent spanning trees rooted at any vertex whenever $C \\log n/n \\leq p < 0.99$. Since the lower bound on $p$ coincides (up to the constant $C$) with the connectivity threshold of $G(n,p)$, this result is essentially optimal. In addition, we show that $(n,d,\\lambda)$-graphs with fairly mild bounds on the spectral ratio $d/\\lambda$ contain $(1-o(1))d$ independent spanning trees rooted at each vertex, thereby settling the conjecture asymptotically for random $d$-regular graphs as well.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe 32nd Workshop on Combinatorial Mathematics and Computation Theory (https://par.cse.nsysu.edu.tw/~algo/paper/paper15/B1_2.pdf)\nciteturn6search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; independent spanning trees∗ ... also given by Pai et al. [6], via a necessary con- ... In this paper, we prove that for graphs of order n, ... ning trees was introduced by Hasunuma [3] in 2001. ... In addition, Ha-\nsunuma [4] gave a conjecture, which states that ... The proof of Theorem 1 is given in Section 3, and\ntheorem.\nIn a graph G, a set of spanning trees are said to\nbe completely independent if for any vertices x and\ny, the paths connecting them on the spanning trees\nhave neither vertex nor edge in common, except\nx and y.\nGraphs discussed in this paper are as-\nsumed to be connected and simple, where a graph\nis simple if it contains neither parallel edge nor\nloop. The concept of completely independent span-\nning trees was introduced by Hasunuma [3] in 2001.\nHe showed that determining whether a graph ad-\nmits k completely independent spanning trees is\nNP-complete, even for k = 2 [4].\nThe construc-\ntion of k completely independent spanning trees\nwas then investigated on some graph classes, with\nconnectivity at least 2k [3–5].\nIn addition, Ha-\nsunuma [4] gave a conjecture, which states that\nbeing 2k-connected is suﬃcient for having k com-\npletely independent spanning trees, but that was\nTheorem 1. Let G be a graph of order n with min-\nimum degree at least n