 ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},\\dots ,{T}_{k}$ are k completely independent spanning trees (CISTs) of G. The concept of CISTs was first introduced by Hasunuma in 2001. In 2002, Hasunuma further proved that determining whether a graph G contains k CISTs is NP-complete, even for $k=2$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe existence condition of k completely independent spanning tree for some graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304397526000599)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In this paper, we first prove the existence of completely independent spanning trees in Hamiltonian graphs based on the number of edges, and then prove the existence of completely independent spanning trees in a graph based on degree conditions. ... Unfortunately, Péterfalvi [6] disproved this conjecture by constructing, for each k ≥ 2, a k-connected graph that does not have two CIST’s. ... Hasunuma [18] discussed the problem of k completely independent spanning trees in a line graph.\nThere is a well known result [3], [4] that every 2k-edge-connected graph has k edge-disjoint spanning trees. Based on this, Hasunuma [5] conjectured that every 2k-connected graph has k CIST’s. Unfortunately, Péterfalvi [6] disproved this conjecture by constructing, for each k ≥ 2, a k-connected graph that does not have two CIST’s. Recently, sufficient conditions have been determined in order to guarantee the existence of two completely independent spanning trees. These conditions are inspired by the sufficient conditions for Hamiltonicity: Fleischner’s condition [7], Dirac’s condition [7], Ore’s condition [8] and Neighborhood Union and Intersection conditions [9]. Moreover, the Diracs condition has been generalized to k( ≥ 2) completely independent spanning trees [10], [11], [12] and has been independently improved [11], [12] for two trees. The Fleischner’s condition has been generaliz